-- ownership: T59, fresh results that share inside one result
-- (`ownership-checker.md` §16.8; thesis Table 6.2). The initializer returns
-- `[ cell, cell ]`, two positions of one new array. Editing one position's
-- inner list through two `List.update`s would be seen at the other, so the
-- program must be rejected: `update`'s summary, inferred from
-- `set xs i (func (unsafeGet xs i))`, supplies its element borrowed, and
-- both lambdas write what they are supplied — rejected, `supply`. The
-- round-5 fix holds by construction: no asserted row hands out a callback's
-- result as exclusive. The thesis accepts `distinct`, two literals being
-- two cells; slice 2, which builds no exclusive contents, rejects it the
-- same way — a finding, not a rule.
pub t59 : Int → Int
t59 n =
    rows = List.initialize 1 λ_ →
        cell = [ 0 ]
        [ cell, cell ]
    changed = List.update rows 0 λpair → List.update pair 0 λxs → [ …xs, 9 ]
    List.length changed + n


pub distinct : Int → Int
distinct n =
    rows = List.initialize 1 λ_ → [ [ 0 ], [ 0 ] ]
    changed = List.update rows 0 λpair → List.update pair 0 λxs → [ …xs, 9 ]
    List.length changed + n
